---
// /compare/ — hub linking every comparison page. Internal-linking + crawl entry
// for the /compare/<slug> set. Prose layout, token-only.
import Prose from '../../layouts/Prose.astro';
import { competitors, COMPARE_UPDATED } from '../../data/compare';
---

<Prose
  title="Compare tela — open-source, self-hosted, AI-native team wiki"
  description="How tela compares to Notion, Confluence, Outline, GitBook, Docmost, Docusaurus, MkDocs, Backstage TechDocs, Mintlify and more — honestly, including what each still does better."
  kicker="Compare"
  heading="Compare tela with the alternatives"
  updated={COMPARE_UPDATED}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@graph': [
      {
        '@type': 'CollectionPage',
        '@id': 'https://telawiki.com/compare/#page',
        name: 'Compare tela with the alternatives',
        url: 'https://telawiki.com/compare/',
        inLanguage: 'en',
        isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://telawiki.com/#website' },
        about: { '@id': 'https://telawiki.com/#software' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
        itemListElement: [
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://telawiki.com/' },
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Compare', item: 'https://telawiki.com/compare/' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  })} />

  <p class="lead">
    tela is an open-source (AGPL), self-hostable, markdown-native team wiki: your
    docs stay as canonical markdown you own — mountable as a folder of
    <code>.md</code> files that syncs both ways — agents read and write them
    through a built-in MCP server, Atlas generates a cited, coverage-checked wiki
    from your git repos and Jira, and any space can be published to the open web.
    Here is how it stacks up against the tools you would otherwise reach for —
    including what each of them still does better.
  </p>

  <ul class="tool-grid">
    {competitors.map((c) => (
      <li>
        <a href={`/compare/${c.slug}/`}>tela vs {c.name}</a>
        <span class="desc">{c.heading}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    Most of these are good tools. tela's distinct angle is three things. It is
    open-source and self-hostable with canonical markdown you own — the same
    pages are <code>.md</code> files in a folder on your disk whenever you want
    them to be. <strong>Atlas</strong> generates documentation from your code and
    then audits itself against the code's real surface, so "undocumented" is a
    number rather than a hunch — which most wikis leave entirely to human
    discipline. And a document, a presentation and a spreadsheet are all just
    pages, still markdown underneath. A built-in MCP server is a strength tela
    shares with several of them, not a monopoly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try it: self-host under AGPL, or start on the free cloud tier at
    <a href="/">telawiki.com</a>.
  </p>
</Prose>
